        "point": "zero_excluded",
        "statement": "The integer 0 is never a member of VTask.roughNumbersUpTo N k for any N and k, because the definition explicitly requires n ≠ 0.",
        "note": "By definition, rough numbers are positive."
      },
      {
        "point": "one_excluded",
        "statement": "The integer 1 is never a member of VTask.roughNumbersUpTo N k for any N and k, because 1 has no prime factors and is therefore vacuously k-smooth for every k.",
        "note": "Follows from the Mathlib convention that 1 ∈ smoothNumbers k for all k."
      },
      {
        "point": "k_below_all_primes",
        "statement": "When k = 0 or k = 1, VTask.roughNumbersUpTo N k equals {2, 3, …, N} (all integers from 2 to N), since every integer ≥ 2 has a prime factor > 1 = k.",
        "note": "Edge case for small thresholds."
      },
      {
        "point": "N_zero",
        "statement": "VTask.roughNumbersUpTo 0 k = ∅ for all k, because there are no positive integers ≤ 0.",
        "note": "Empty-range edge case."
      }
    ]
  },
